            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Removes axes of length 1 from a tensor's shape, leaving the numbers alone.\n\nA column of twelve values has shape [12, 1]; a row that came out of a batch has shape [1, 12]. Both hold the same twelve numbers as a plain [12], but many nodes treat them differently - a [12, 1] is a 2-D table with one column, not a list. t.squeeze drops those stray 1s.\n\nWHICH AXES:\nWith no argument, EVERY axis of length 1 goes: [1, 12, 1] becomes [12]. With an argument (t.squeeze 0), only that axis is considered, and only if its length really is 1 - otherwise the tensor passes through unchanged. Naming the axis is safer in a patch whose sizes can change: a batch of one squeezes away as you want, but so would a list that happens to hold one value.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.squeeze\nt.squeeze <dim: int>\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.squeeze 0\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor (a list or numpy array becomes a float32 tensor first). Receiving it makes the node send.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet - only when an argument was given):\nThe one axis to remove. Negative counts from the end (-1 is the last). An axis the tensor does not have sends nothing. Changing it does NOT send by itself; the next tensor uses it. A bare t.squeeze has no dim box: retype it with an argument to get one.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe same numbers, with the length-1 axes gone.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe ramp of twelve is folded by t.reshape into a column, [12, 1] - twelve rows of one value. t.squeeze removes the 1: the shape is [12] and the heat_map draws it as a row. t.squeeze 0 only looks at axis 0, which has length 12, so it changes nothing ([12, 1] still). Click 1 12: now both remove a 1 (axis 0 is the length-1 one). Click 3 4: nothing to remove, both pass it through.\n\nRELATED:\nt.unsqueeze adds a length-1 axis - the reverse. t.reshape can set any shape. t.flatten collapses every axis, not just the length-1 ones. np.squeeze is the numpy version.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 880,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Removes axes of length 1 from a tensor's shape, leaving the numbers alone.\n\nA column of twelve values has shape [12, 1]; a row that came out of a batch has shape [1, 12]. Both hold the same twelve numbers as a plain [12], but many nodes treat them differently - a [12, 1] is a 2-D table with one column, not a list. t.squeeze drops those stray 1s.\n\nWHICH AXES:\nWith no argument, EVERY axis of length 1 goes: [1, 12, 1] becomes [12]. With an argument (t.squeeze 0), only that axis is considered, and only if its length really is 1 - otherwise the tensor passes through unchanged. Naming the axis is safer in a patch whose sizes can change: a batch of one squeezes away as you want, but so would a list that happens to hold one value.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.squeeze\nt.squeeze <dim: int>\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.squeeze 0\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor (a list or numpy array becomes a float32 tensor first). Receiving it makes the node send.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet - only when an argument was given):\nThe one axis to remove. Negative counts from the end (-1 is the last). An axis the tensor does not have sends nothing. Changing it does NOT send by itself; the next tensor uses it. A bare t.squeeze has no dim box: retype it with an argument to get one.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe same numbers, with the length-1 axes gone.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe ramp of twelve is folded by t.reshape into a column, [12, 1] - twelve rows of one value. t.squeeze removes the 1: the shape is [12] and the heat_map draws it as a row. t.squeeze 0 only looks at axis 0, which has length 12, so it changes nothing ([12, 1] still). Click 1 12: now both remove a 1 (axis 0 is the length-1 one). Click 3 4: nothing to remove, both pass it through.\n\nRELATED:\nt.unsqueeze adds a length-1 axis - the reverse. t.reshape can set any shape. t.flatten collapses every axis, not just the length-1 ones. np.squeeze is the numpy version."
        },
